#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Sort every part: what it is (the UI Hub's group/category), a finer subcategory, and the facets beside it.

Shaan, 10 Oct: "start going through them and re-categorizing them way more detailed … categories are pretty good maybe
they should be broken up and subcategories more and rated as well and which ones are duplicates".

From first principles (ECOSYSTEM read twenty parts by hand first, 10 Oct):
- A part is a base or a variant of one. Examples, demos and numbered or styled variants ("alert-outlined-secondary",
  "v-popover-4", "tooltip-placement") belong to their base, which shows them; they are not cards of their own.
- What a part IS is one category from the hub's own taxonomy (group/category, so the hub's nav and crumbs keep working),
  then a subcategory where a category is crowded (buttons: animated, icon, social; backgrounds: shader, particles…).
- Beside that: the framework (React, Vue, Svelte, ReScript), the style (animated, 3D, WebGL, glass, HUD…) read from
  dependencies and words, and the domain (AI, auth, billing, commerce, crypto, maps, email, dev tools).
- Non-visual parts get their own places: hooks and utilities (lib), themes, and assets (icons, logos, sounds, fonts).
- Numbered names ("Hero 6", "Glitch 002") get a display name from their category and library.
- Copies: parts whose files are byte-identical after whitespace share a dup id (needs the code on disk; else skipped).

  bin/classify                 # catalog.jsonl + sites.jsonl -> classified.jsonl, and taxonomy.json (the vocabulary with counts)
  bin/classify --sample 40     # print 40 random rows, to read
  overrides.jsonl              # a reader's call on one part ({"id", "cat", "sub"?, "by"}), applied over the rules

How the rules read a name (ECOSYSTEM, 10 Oct, sixty parts read by hand, 13 of 20 right before, 19 of 20 after):
- what a part is FOR outranks its shape: assets and AI words in the name or title win first;
- then the head of the name: in English the last word names the thing ("accordion gallery" is a gallery), after the
  words that say nothing (demo, 01, section, primary) are dropped; "X with Y" is an X; a repeated prefix
  ("button-button-text-marquee", shadcnblocks) is the category; a compound the rules know whole wins ("context menu");
  a container ("pricing card", "logo grid", "login form") is named by what it holds, but never by its look; a weak
  head ("date range picker") takes its longest match; an effect on a thing ("footer brand backdrop") is the thing;
- a section ("Items Section ... for features or benefits") is read as marketing first;
- then the old reading (name, title, the registry's categories and file path, in rule order), then the description.
"""
import argparse, collections, hashlib, json, os, random, re, subprocess, sys

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)))

# ---- what it is: (pattern over the part's name text, group/category); first match wins, specific before general ----
